"[1202.1350] Verifiable Computation with Massively Parallel Interactive Proofs (https://arxiv.org/abs/1202.1350)\nciteturn6view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1202.1350\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 173\nL0: 【0†Search】 【1†Submit】 【2†Donate†info.arxiv.org】 【3†Log in】\nL1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · 【4†Advanced search】\nL5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 7 Feb 2012 (【5†v1】), last revised 22 Feb 2012 (this version, v3)]\nL9: # Title:Verifiable Computation with Massively Parallel Interactive Proofs\nL10: \nL11: Authors:【6†Justin Thaler】, 【7†Mike Roberts】, 【8†Michael Mitzenmacher】, 【9†Hanspeter Pfister】\nL12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Verifiable Computation with Massively Parallel Interactive Proofs, by Justin Thaler and 3 other authors\nL14: \nL15: 【10†View PDF】 【11†HTML (experimental)】\nL16: > Abstract:As the cloud computing paradigm has gained prominence, the need for verifiable computation has grown increasingly urgent. The concept of verifiable computation enables a weak client to outsource difficult computations to a powerful, but untrusted, server. Protocols for verifiable computation aim to provide the client with a guarantee that the server performed the requested computations correctly, without requiring the client to perform the computations herself.\nL17: By design, these protocols impose a minimal computational burden on the client. However, existing protocols require the server to perform a large amount of extra bookkeeping in order to enable a client to easily verify the results. Verifiable computation has thus remained a theoretical curiosity, and protocols for it have not been implemented in real cloud computing systems.\nL18: > Our goal is to leverage GPUs to reduce the server-side slowdown for verifiable computation. To this end, we identify abundant data parallelism in a state-of-the-art general-purpose protocol for verifiable computation, originally due to Goldwasser, Kalai, and Rothblum, and recently extended by Cormode, Mitzenmacher, and Thaler. We implement this protocol on the GPU, obtaining 40-120x server-side speedups relative to a state-of-the-art sequential implementation.\nL19: For benchmark problems, our implementation reduces the slowdown of the server to factors of 100-500x relative to the original computations requested by the client. Furthermore, we reduce the already small runtime of the client by 100x. Similarly, we obtain 20-50x server-side and client-side speedups for related protocols targeted at specific streaming problems.\nL20: We believe our results demonstrate the immediate practicality of using GPUs for verifiable computation, and more generally that protocols for verifiable computation have become sufficiently mature to deploy in real cloud computing systems.\nL21: Comments:  | 17 pages, 6 figures, 3 tables\nL22: Subjects:  | Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC); Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)\nL23: Cite as:  | 【12†arXiv:1202.1350】 [cs.DC]\nL24:    | (or 【13†arXiv:1202.1350v3】 [cs.DC] for this version)\nL25:    | 【14†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.1202.1350†doi.org】 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL26: ## Submission history\nL27: \nL28: From: Justin Thaler [【15†view email】]\nL29: 【5†[v1]】 Tue, 7 Feb 2012 04:42:49 UTC (1,607 KB)\nL30: 【16†[v2]】 Tue, 14 Feb 2012 05:42:28 UTC (1,609 KB)\nL31: [v3] Wed, 22 Feb 2012 04:15:37 UTC (1,602 KB)\nL32: \nL33: Full-text links:\nL34: \nL35: ## Access Paper:\nL36: \nL37: View a PDF of the paper titled Verifiable Computation with Massively Parallel Interactive Proofs, by Justin Thaler and 3 other authors\nL38: \nL39:   * 【10†View PDF】\nL40:   * 【11†HTML (experimental)】\nL41:   * 【17†TeX Source】\nL42: \nL43: 【18†view license】\nL44: ### Current browse context:\nL45: \nL46: cs.DC\nL47: \nL48: 【19†< prev】   |   【20†next >】\nL49: \nL50: 【21†new】 | 【22†recent】 | 【23†2012-02】\nL51: \nL52: Change to browse by:\nL53: \nL54: 【24†cs】\nL55: 【25†cs.CR】\nL56: \nL57: ### References & Citations\nL58: \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://www.usenix.org/system/files/conference/hotcloud12/hotcloud12-final8.pdf)\nciteturn6view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 6; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.usenix.org/system/files/conference/hotcloud12/hotcloud12-final8.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 508\nL0@P0: Verifiable Computation with Massively Parallel\nL1@P0: Interactive Proofs\nL2@P0: Justin Thaler∗, Mike Roberts∗, Michael Mitzenmacher∗, and Hanspeter Pfister∗\nL3@P0: ∗Harvard University, School of Engineering and Applied Sciences\nL4@P0: Abstract— As the cloud computing paradigm has gained\nL5@P0: prominence, the need for verifiable computation has grown\nL6@P0: increasingly urgent. Protocols for verifiable computation\nL7@P0: enable a weak client to outsource difficult computations to\nL8@P0: a powerful, but untrusted server, in a way that provides\nL9@P0: the client with a guarantee that the server performed\nL10@P0: the requested computations correctly. By design, these\nL11@P0: protocols impose a minimal computational burden on the\nL12@P0: client, but they require the server to perform a very large\nL13@P0: amount of extra bookkeeping to enable a client to easily\nL14@P0: verify the results. Verifiable computation has thus remained\nL15@P0: a theoretical curiosity, and protocols for it have not been\nL16@P0: implemented in real cloud computing systems.\nL17@P0: In this paper, we assess the potential of parallel process\u0002ing to help make practical verification a reality, identifying\nL18@P0: abundant data parallelism in a state-of-the-art general\u0002purpose protocol for verifiable computation. We implement\nL19@P0: this protocol on the GPU, obtaining 40-120× server-side\nL20@P0: speedups relative to a state-of-the-art sequential imple\u0002mentation. For benchmark problems, our implementation\nL21@P0: thereby reduces the slowdown of the server to within\nL22@P0: factors of 100-500× relative to the original computations\nL23@P0: requested by the client. Furthermore, we reduce the al\u0002ready small runtime of the client by 100×. Our results\nL24@P0: demonstrate the immediate practicality of using GPUs for\nL25@P0: verifiable computation, and more generally, that protocols\nL26@P0: for verifiable computation have become sufficiently mature\nL27@P0: to deploy in real cloud computing systems.\nL28@P0: I. INTRODUCTION\nL29@P0: A potential problem in outsourcing work to commer\u0002cial cloud computing services is trust. If we store a large\nL30@P0: dataset with a server, and ask the server to perform a\nL31@P0: computation on that dataset – for example, to compute\nL32@P0: the eigenvalues of a large graph – how can we know\nL33@P0: the computation was performed correctly? We don’t\nL34@P0: want to compute the result ourselves, and we might not\nL35@P0: even be able to store all the data locally. Despite these\nL36@P0: constraints, we would like the server to both provide us\nL37@P0: with the answer and convince us the answer is correct.\nL38@P0: Protocols for verifiable computation offer a possible\nL39@P0: solution. The primary goal is to enable the client to\nL40@P0: obtain results with a guarantee of correctness from the\nL41@P0: server much more efficiently than performing the com\u0002putations herself. Another important goal is to enable\nL42@P0: the server to provide guarantees of correctness almost as\nL43@P0: efficiently as providing results without such guarantees.\nL44@P0: Interactive proofs are protocols for establishing guar\u0002antees of correctness between a client and server. Al\u0002though they have been studied in the theory community\nL45@P0: for decades, there has been no significant effort to imple\u0002ment or deploy such proof systems until very recently.\nL46@P0: However, a recent line of work substantially advanced\nL47@P0: the practicality of these techniques. In particular, the\nL48@P0: work of Cormode, Mitzenmacher, and Thaler [3] demon\u0002strates that: (1) a powerful general-purpose methodology\nL49@P0: due to Goldwasser, Kalai and Rothblum [5] approaches\nL50@P0: practicality; and (2) special-purpose protocols for a large\nL51@P0: class of streaming problems are already practical.\nL52@P0: We take things a key step further by leveraging\nL53@P0: the parallelism offered by GPUs to obtain significant\nL54@P0: speedups relative to state-of-the-art implementations of\nL55@P0: [3]. We expect that practical verification protocols will\nL56@P0: have to be utilized eventually if cloud computing solu\u0002tions are to be widely adopted for correctness-critical\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndraft-irtf-cfrg-longfellow-00 - Longfellow ZK (https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-irtf-cfrg-longfellow/)\nciteturn6view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-irtf-cfrg-longfellow/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 3580\nL0: 【0†Site status】\nL1:   * 【1†Groups】\nL2:     * By area/parent\nL3:     * 【2†Apps & Realtime】\nL4:     * 【3†General】\nL5:     * 【4†Internet】\nL6:     * 【5†Ops & Management】\nL7:     * 【6†Routing】\nL8:     * 【7†Security】\nL9:     * 【8†Web and Internet Transport】\nL10:     * 【9†IESG】\nL11:     * 【10†IAB】\nL12:     * 【11†IRTF】\nL13:     * 【12†IETF LLC】\nL14:     * 【13†RFC Editor】\nL15:     * 【14†Other】\nL16:       * 【15†Active AGs】\nL17:       * 【16†Active Areas】\nL18:       * 【17†Active Directorates】\nL19:       * 【18†Active IAB Workshops】\nL20:       * 【10†Active Programs】\nL21:       * 【19†Active RAGs】\nL22:       * 【20†Active Teams】\nL23:     * * * *\nL24:     * New work\nL25: 